WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:13.380
Every AEI agent tutorial you have watched has lined to you. They show you one agent doing one task in a straight line, prompt in, answer out, looks great in a demo, and you try to build something real.

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:15.460
Five agents, one goal,

00:00:15.855 --> 00:00:28.355
And it falls apart. Agents talk over each other. State gets lost. One agent loops forever and burns your API while you are asleep. There is a reason for that, and there is a fix. It is called graph engineering.

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:45.890
And once you see it, you cannot unsee it. Here's the mental shift. A single AEI agent is not one call to a model. It is a loop. Think. Act. Look at the result. Think again. You cannot draw. A loop with an arrow that only points forward, a straight line cannot come back on itself. So the moment you need looping

00:00:46.384 --> 00:00:49.204
or branching or five agents working at once,

00:00:49.664 --> 00:00:52.564
straight chain breaks, you need graph, nodes,

00:00:53.504 --> 00:00:59.364
edges, and something flowing between them. Every graph based agent system runs on four things. One,

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:04.769
state, shared object every agent reads and writes to. Not the conversation,

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:10.609
real structured data, the task, the plan, the results so far, and the errors. Two,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.944
nodes, each one does one job, an LLM call, tool call, or human checkpoint.

00:01:16.405 --> 00:01:18.825
Nodes can contain entire agents inside them.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:20.744
Nesting like Russian dolls.

00:01:21.204 --> 00:01:23.384
Three, edges, conditional edges,

00:01:23.844 --> 00:01:27.465
function that looks at the state and asks what happens next. This

00:01:27.924 --> 00:01:28.664
right here

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.420
is the actual engineering,

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:33.299
not the prompts, the routing, and four,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.340
checkpoints,

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:46.954
safe state after every step. When an agent runs for ten minutes and step 41 crashes, you do not wanna lose steps one through 40. Okay. Here's how you actually go from one agent to an army. Pattern one, the supervisor.

00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:51.834
Picture a general and five soldiers. The soldiers never talk to each other.

00:01:52.295 --> 00:01:55.674
They report back to the general. Who decides who moves next?

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.480
One brain, many hands. Pattern two,

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:03.840
fan out, fan in. This is the real army move. Say you need to research five competitors.

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:07.840
You do not send one agent to do it five times in. A row.

00:02:08.314 --> 00:02:23.969
You spawn five agents at once. Each takes one competitor, and they all report back to single reducer that merges the results. But here is the trap almost everyone falls into. If two agents write to the same piece of state at the same time and you have not told your system how to merge them,

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:26.949
One silently erases the other's work.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.870
Nobody warns you about this.

00:02:29.169 --> 00:02:32.150
I am warning you. Pattern three, hierarchical.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.389
Once you are past five or six agents, one supervisor gets overwhelmed.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.030
So supervisors get their own sub supervisors,

00:02:39.485 --> 00:02:40.305
teams of

00:02:40.604 --> 00:02:51.745
teams. That is how you actually scale this past a demo. Three things will bite you the first time you build this for real, loots that never end. Because you asked an AI, are we done yet? And it cannot decide.

00:02:52.044 --> 00:02:54.705
Fix, always cap it with hard iteration limit.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.560
Cost that quietly explodes

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.400
because every hop through your graph is full model call and debugging.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:07.520
In graph, you must log every state change at every step where you will be flying blind. These are not edge cases. These are Tuesday.

00:03:07.824 --> 00:03:26.590
So here is the whole idea in one image. Stop thinking about your agents as a line. Start thinking about them as a map with shared memory, decision points at every fork, and plan for what happens when five pass need to become one. The agents you have been building, they were never the hard part, the coordination between them.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:33.469
That is the actual skill. That is the part nobody teaches, and now you know it. If this made something click for you,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.069
go build your army.
